  "issue_number": 16083,
  "issue_title": "Lint/SafeNavigationConsistency: false positive on `a && x&.foo || b && x&.foo`",
  "issue_body": "### Before submitting\n\n- [x] I have searched the existing issues and this hasn't been reported yet.\n- [x] I am running the latest stable version of RuboCop.\n\n### Issue type\n\nFalse positive\n\n### Cop name\n\nLint/SafeNavigationConsistency\n\n### Steps to reproduce the problem\n\nCreate a Ruby file with the following contents:\n\n```ruby\n# frozen_string_literal: true\n\ndef foo(id, str)\n  id == 'x' && str&.empty? || id == 'y' && str&.empty?\nend\n```\nand run:\n\n```shell\nrubocop --only Lint/SafeNavigationConsistency example.rb\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nNo offense.\nThe safe navigation operator on the second `str&.empty?` is required: when `id == 'y'`, the left operand of `||` is falsy because `id == 'x'` is false, so `str` has never been evaluated and can still be `nil`.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\n```\nexample.rb:4:47: E: [Correctable] Lint/SafeNavigationConsistency: Use . instead of unnecessary &..\n  id == 'x' && str&.empty? || id == 'y' && str&.empty?\n```\nApplying the suggested fix (`str.empty?` in the second branch) changes the behavior:\n\n```ruby\ndef foo(id, str)\n  id == 'x' && str&.empty? || id == 'y' && str.empty?\nend\n```\n\nThe cop seems to assume that the first `&.` guarantees the receiver is non-nil in the second branch. That holds for `&&`, but not when the two usages are in different branches of an `||`.\n\n### RuboCop configuration\n\n```yaml\n\n```\n\n### RuboCop version\n\n```shell\n$ rubocop -V\n1.92.0 (using Parser 3.3.12.0, Prism 1.8.1, rubocop-ast 1.50.0, analyzing as Ruby 4.0, running on ruby 4.0.6) [x86_64-linux]\n  - rubocop-minitest 0.41.0\n  - rubocop-performance 1.27.0\n```",
  "merged_at": "2026-10-15T18:10:01Z",
